{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Two tenons for each board.</b> Rashi wrote (Rashi on Exodus 26:17): he would groove the board from below at its midpoint, one cubit in height, and leave a quarter of its width on one side and a quarter of its width on the other side, and these are the tenons, and the groove in the middle of the tenons is half the width of the board. And those tenons he inserts into the sockets, which were hollow, and the sockets were one cubit high and placed contiguously, forty next to forty, and the tenons of the boards that enter the hollow of the sockets are grooved on three of their sides, the width of the groove being equal to the thickness of the socket's lip, so that the board covers the entire top of the socket — for if not, there would be a gap between board and board equal to the thickness of the lips of the two sockets that intervene between them. And this is what is stated (Exodus 26:24), 'And they shall be matched from below' — that he should groove the tenons on their sides so that the boards join one to the other. This is the language of the Rav [Maimonides] that I found, and I am astonished by it: for if the groove in the middle were half the width of the board — which is four and a half handbreadths — it would require that the thickness of the socket's lip be a quarter of the board's width, which is two handbreadths and a thumb-breadth, so that the thickness of the two sockets fills the groove in the middle, which is half the board's width, and there would be no gap between socket and socket. And if so, when you groove the boards on three of their sides to that extent, nothing at all would remain of them, and the lips of the sockets would be equal on every side. Furthermore, the proof that the Rav cited is as it is taught in the baraita of the Making of the Tabernacle: 'The order of the boards for the work of the Tabernacle — he would make the sockets hollow, and groove the board from below, a quarter on this side and a quarter on that side, and groove half of it in the middle, and make for it two tenons like two rungs, and insert them into the two sockets.' This baraita too, according to its plain meaning, contains a great difficulty: for it grooves from the board a quarter on this side and a quarter on that side, and in the middle it makes a groove of half the width of the board — the result is that the entire board is grooved, with not a hand or foot remaining at all. But in my opinion this baraita did not specify a measurement for the groove, because the Torah did not specify a measurement for the thickness of the sockets' lips. Therefore they said that he would groove the board, a quarter of it grooved on this side to cover one lip of the socket, and a quarter grooved on that side, and in the middle of the board he would groove half of the entire groove in that board, to cover the lips of the two sockets. And according to everyone's words, analysis is required [to show] that there was not at the bottom of the Tabernacle, one cubit high, a width of ten cubits — since the thickness of the two sockets on either side diminishes it — but perhaps there is no strict concern about this. And according to Rashi's words, the board, whose thickness was one cubit, he would groove in the thickness of the tenons from the inside according to the thickness of the sockets' lips. And this is correct, except that this was not mentioned in the baraita." "<b>Joined, one to another.</b> Made like the rungs of a ladder. 'One to another' — aligned opposite each other, and separated one from the other, and smoothed at their heads to enter into the hollow of the socket like a tenon that enters into the holes of a ladder's uprights, and they shall be equal grooves, each the measure of the other, so that the two tenons are not drawn — one toward the inside and one toward the outside — within the thickness of the boards, which is one cubit. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 26:17). Now according to his words, 'joined' refers to the tenons — that they should be smoothed at their heads to enter the hollow of the sockets — and 'one to another' applies only to the boards, that they should be evenly joined. But 'boards' is a masculine noun, and it should have said 'each man to his brother' [איש אל אחיו]. Perhaps, however, it says 'one woman to her sister' [אשה אל אחתה] because the tenons are aligned opposite each other and their grooves are equal, for through this the boards become joined. And the tenons — their heads are not literally tapered, since the hollow of the sockets is uniform throughout the socket; rather, there is a groove on their sides so that board joins to board. But in the baraita of the Making of the Tabernacle I found: 'And the two tenons emerge from the boards, two for each and every one; for he inserts the male into the female, as it is stated: <b>joined, one to another</b>' — the words of Rabbi Nechemyah. For Rabbi Nechemyah says: it is not necessary for the verse to say מְשֻׁלָּבוֹת; and what does the verse mean by מְשֻׁלָּבוֹת? That he makes for them rungs like an Egyptian ladder. And the meaning of tenons [סִנִּין] is like what is made for chests, to tighten and join the boards one to another, like wooden pegs, as we learned in the Mishnah, Kelim (10:6): 'If he made them with tenons [סִנִּין] or with dowels [שֹׁגְמִין], it does not require lining in the middle'; and it says in the Talmud (Bava Batra 117a) 'reeds [קָנִים] and tenons [וּסְנָאִין].' According to this, it would appear that the verse says he should make a tenon protruding from the board like male pegs, and opposite it, in the board beside it, another tenon that is hollow, and he inserts the male into the female — and likewise for the other board, so that each board has two tenons entering one into the other. <b>Joined, one to another</b> — this is a name for the tenons themselves, and it is a feminine form here. And so the Sages referred to them: 'a tenon slipped from beneath it' (Makkot 7a), 'a ladder after its rungs' (Shabbat 60a). And although we find 'between the rungs' (I Kings 7:28), that is like 'women and concubines' [masculine form used for feminine referents]." "<b>And the meaning of 'on the east side, eastward'.</b> (Exodus 27:13) — for the sacred tongue calls the east קֶדֶם ['front'], as in 'and they shall measure outside the city, the eastern side' (Ezekiel 45:6), and it calls the west אָחוֹר ['behind'], as in 'Behold, I go forward [קֶדֶם] but He is not there, and backward [וְאָחוֹר] but I cannot perceive Him' (Job 23:8), and likewise 'to the sea behind' ['עַד הַיָּם הָאַחֲרוֹן'] (Deuteronomy 34:2) — the western one. And both are epithets, for language takes epithets from a person facing the sunlight. And likewise it says 'and they turned backward [לְאָחוֹר] and not forward' (Jeremiah 7:24). And it calls the south 'נֶגֶב,' because it is dried out [נָגוּב] on account of the heat. And sometimes Scripture mentions the epithet and clarifies it with the proper name: it says 'קֵדְמָה' ['eastward'], which is an epithet, and clarifies 'מִזְרָחָה' ['eastward'], which is the name. And it says 'נֶגְבָּה' ['southward'], as an epithet, and clarifies 'תֵּימָנָה' ['southward'], which is the name. And the west it mentions by epithet, 'sea' ['יָם'] (Exodus 26:22), because it describes it in terms of the people of the Land of Israel, for whom the Great Sea is to the west. And the north it mentions (Exodus 26:20) by its proper name. And it is called thus because it is צָפוּן ['hidden'] — the sun is never seen in it. And the south is called דָרוֹם, as in 'going toward the south [דָרוֹם] and turning toward the north [צָפוֹן]' (Ecclesiastes 1:6) — and it is a compound word, missing one רֵי\"שׁ to join two identical letters: דָר רוּם ['dwelling of height'], for the sun travels at the height of that direction. And the south is called 'right' [יָמִין] and the north 'left' [שְׂמֹאל] (see Isaiah 54:3 and its Targum), as epithets for a person facing east, as I have mentioned.\n\nAnd the secret of these names is known from the workings of the supernal Chariot. And likewise the name of the west, 'sea' [יָם], is from what they said: the Shekhinah is in the west (Bava Batra 25a), for the sea is the wisdom of Solomon, as they said in the Midrash: 'sea' means nothing but Torah, as it is stated (Job 11:9), 'and broader than the sea [יָם].' And I am destined to mention this in the portion of Ve-Zot Ha-Berakhah, on the verse 'the sea and the south [יָם וְדָרוֹם] he shall inherit' (Deuteronomy 33:23), if my Rock will bless me to reach there."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {1 "Ramban cites Bava Batra 117a; the abbreviation ב\"מ could in principle refer to Bava Metzia, but the passage about קנים וסנאין is found in Bava Batra."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:17:35.633984731Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}